best friends whatever while something on  my face  hi everybody its senior editor Kevin van  ORD standing in for reviewer nathan ba  to tell you about jazzpunk the promise  of getting deluge by weirdness awaits  your every move in jazzpunk a cartoony  cyberpunk first-person adventure that  attempts to shoehorn as many funny  moments as possible into its two-hour  length this off-the-wall journey does  not follow a straight line from start to  finish however since every retro  futurists etting you explore is riddled  with crazy secrets to discover oh good  boobs ah jazz punks warped version of  the special agent life begins with its  silent protagonist Polly Blanc being  mailed through customs in a human-shaped  suitcase and deposited on the doorstep  of a top-secret espionage agency that's  based out of an abandoned subway car if  the brain warping neons psychedelic  intro doesn't make you feel like you've  been drugged the ebb and flow of the  many humorous absurdities layered thick  throughout the peculiar opening moments  certainly will  from there the wild ride takes a more  overt turn towards the bizarre when your  director hands you a prescription bottle  of mysterious pills to take in order to  be quote-unquote transported to your  first mission yeah that's all just in  the first minute or so  the surreal missions that follow have  you degaussing and smuggling pigeons  extracting mechanical organs from sushi  loving Cowboys cross-dressing for a  vacation rendezvous briefcase swap  murdering a bionic Pig with a six string  guitar and even photocopying your  backside it's all excessively bizarre  which is a huge part of the fun you  never know what crazy thing you'll  encounter next and being diligent in  poking around every nook and cranny  rewards you with a slew of hilarious  secrets and gags overcoming obstacles in  your way and sniffing out the path ahead  isn't particularly challenging though  the often unusual nature of the many  tasks you have to complete to progress  makes them highly entertaining to fiddle  through experimenting to see what  happens when you interact with an object  or test out some new gizmo you've  acquired on an unwitting test subject  is often rewarding on its own humor is  the heart of this demented adventure and  it's hard not to chuckle a bit when  you're spraying liquid cheese into the  mouth of a bespectacled gentleman or  beheading pepperoni and cheese zombies  with the giant pizza cutter the  situations you find yourself in from one  moment to the next row increasingly  outrageous the further you push not all  of the gags hit the mark though and the  laughs worthy mileage you get will  depend in part on your amusement  threshold for constant wisecracks  Jakub retro themes minigames modeled  after classics from the 8-bit and 16-bit  eras throw added variety into this crazy  concoction they're entertaining asides  that break up the gag heavy exploration  and minor puzzle elements though  jazzpunk leans on these diversions too  heavily at times but by the time you're  playing mini-golf and racing gravy boats  towards the final stretches the  substance and humor have wanes  considerably  it's an odd shift considering the  overall level of ingenious creativity  thrown in elsewhere the bonus  multiplayer mode wedding-cake is one big  exception however having a let's get  married  deathmatch where you engage opponents  and blast them with champagne corks good  time baby  one spring even if the humor doesn't  always click and the depth can be light  at moments jazzpunk stylish presentation  and great backing soundtrack set a slick  atmosphere the cool vibe inspires the  need to stick around in its peculiar  world a bit longer jazzpunk is an  enthusiastic attempt to answer the  question of just how much weirdness can  you possibly cram into a few hours of  gaming and in that endeavor it's a great  achievement  you
 

